---
/**
 * How Game on Paper Pro's numbers are made (plan 3.5). Readable without signing in
 * (middleware.ts lets it past the entitlement check), and linked from every PDF
 * footer. Every statement here is the code's: gop-pro bands.py (shrinkage, bands,
 * the no-band rule), build.py (snapshots, the receiving-data rule, prior-season
 * deltas, PFF coverage) and export.py (what a profile shows, PFF families, the
 * coverage notes). Change those and this page changes with them.
 *
 * Not prerendered on purpose: a prerendered page is a static asset, served before
 * the Worker runs, so the 'pro' flag could not hide it.
 */
import GenericPage from '../../layouts/GenericPage.astro';
import ProNav from '../../components/pro/ProNav.astro';
import { PRO_SEASON } from '../../resources/pro';
---
<GenericPage title="Methodology | Game on Paper Pro" subtitle="How the Pro numbers are made" slug="/pro/methodology"
    breadcrumbs={[{ title: 'Pro', url: '/pro', active: false }, { title: 'Methodology', active: true }]} heading="Game on Paper Pro methodology">
    <ProNav active="methodology" />
    <div class="container mb-4">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-lg-8 col-12">
                <h1 class="fs-3">Methodology</h1>
                <p>
                    Pro profiles start from the same play-by-play numbers as the free site (EPA, success rate and the rest are in
                    the <a href="/glossary">glossary</a>) and add three things: small samples shrunk toward the position average,
                    a percentile band instead of a single rank, and a note on everything the data can't support.
                    Valuation (below) turns the same numbers into model dollar values.
                </p>

                <h2 class="fs-4 mt-4">Percentile bands</h2>
                <p>
                    A band is an 80% interval: where the player's true rate probably sits, written as percentiles of the
                    qualifying players at his position. The tick is the middle estimate. The shaded part runs from 1.28
                    standard deviations below it to 1.28 above, so a player with few plays gets a wide band and a starter
                    gets a narrow one.
                </p>

                <h2 class="fs-4 mt-4">Shrinkage toward the position average</h2>
                <p>
                    A rate over a handful of plays is mostly noise, so each player's rate is pulled toward the play-weighted
                    average of his position group, harder the fewer plays he has. The estimate is
                    <code>(n &times; rate + k &times; average) / (n + k)</code> for <code>n</code> plays: the prior is worth
                    <code>k</code> plays. <code>k</code> is the play-to-play noise divided by the real spread between players,
                    and both are fitted each season, per position group and metric, from that season's own players. For a
                    yes/no rate such as success rate the noise follows from the average itself. Players with fewer than 5
                    plays stay out of the fit, and a group needs at least 10 players to get one.
                </p>

                <h2 class="fs-4 mt-4">Among qualifiers</h2>
                <p>
                    Percentiles rank a player against the qualifiers at his position only: players with enough plays for
                    the published leaderboards to rank them. A player below that line still gets a band, read against the
                    qualifiers, and his profile says the band is wide. Metrics with fewer than 5 plays are not shown at all.
                </p>

                <h2 class="fs-4 mt-4">When there is no band</h2>
                <p>
                    If the prior is worth more plays than even the busiest player at the position ran, the season can't
                    move anyone off the average, and a band would only restate it. Those metrics say "no band". In 2025
                    that was running back receiving EPA (no measurable spread between players) and running back rushing EPA
                    (the prior was worth about 620 carries; the most anyone had was 295).
                </p>

                <h2 class="fs-4 mt-4">Receiving data, 2021&ndash;24, and comparing seasons</h2>
                <p>
                    ESPN's play data names the targeted receiver on 69&ndash;82% of targets for 2021&ndash;24 (53&ndash;89%
                    earlier) and on 95% or more from 2025. The missing ones are mostly incompletions, so before 2025 targets
                    are undercounted and catch rate and EPA per target run high. Per-target receiving rates for 2021&ndash;24
                    are not published, and no profile prints a receiving change across the 2024 to 2025 line: it would
                    measure the data, not the player.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Other changes compare a player with his own previous season, on any team, so transfers count. For a
                    mid-season mover that is his busier stint. The change is in the shrunk estimate.
                </p>

                <h2 class="fs-4 mt-4">PFF figures</h2>
                <p>
                    Grades and charting figures are from <a href="https://www.pff.com">PFF</a>, used under license, and are
                    PFF's own estimates. We rank them, as a percentile among players rated at the same PFF position (100 or
                    more snaps), but we don't band them. They cover part of the game for some positions: pass blocking for
                    offensive linemen, the pass rush for defensive linemen, and coverage for linebackers and defensive backs.
                    Run blocking, run defense and tackling are not included. A player not matched to PFF, or under 100
                    snaps, has no grade, and his profile says so. We sell analysis, not PFF's data: no raw PFF export
                    leaves the site, and the portal board's CSV carries derived columns only.
                </p>

                <h2 class="fs-4 mt-4">Data sources and refresh</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Production and percentiles</strong>: <a href="https://www.sportsdataverse.org">SportsDataverse</a>'s
                        ESPN college football passing, rushing, receiving, percentile and roster releases, plus its recruiting
                        and team portal releases.
                    </li>
                    <li><strong>PFF season figures</strong>: through the SportsDataverse Data API, matched to ESPN player ids.</li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Refresh</strong>: each build reads one pinned snapshot of those inputs, copied with checksums, so
                        a report can be rebuilt exactly. Every page and PDF shows its snapshot id and the date it was rendered.
                    </li>
                </ul>

                <h2 class="fs-4 mt-4" id="valuation">Valuation</h2>
                <p>
                    Every player gets two numbers. <strong>Production value</strong> is what his play this season is worth to a program:
                    his value above a typical backup, in EPA, at a full-season pace. <strong>Projected value</strong> is next season's
                    expected value as a starter, with an 80% range. The gap between them is regression risk.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Positional value</strong>: team EPA per play regressed on each unit's quality (2023-26), each coefficient
                        pulled toward its side's average by its standard error. The offensive line also gets its team's run-blocking
                        environment from our rushing-over-expected model.</li>
                    <li><strong>Dollars</strong>: a P4 program's revenue-share pool for football (the House settlement cap, growing 4% a
                        year, 75% to football), split half by on-field value and half by starting slots, then within a unit by each
                        player's share of its value. Model estimates, not reported deals, and no third-party NIL.</li>
                    <li><strong>Projection</strong>: next season's percentile from this season's, a talent prior (position, star rating,
                        year in college) and years in college. Checked out of sample, it beats "same as this year", but the ranges are wide.</li>
                    <li><strong>Age</strong>: a recruiting-class proxy, within a year of the birth date for 94% of players who have one.</li>
                    <li><strong>Scarcity and replace odds</strong>: P4 starting jobs opened per starter-caliber portal entrant last cycle,
                        and the chance a program lands an equal entrant, from its share of portal commits at that recruiting tier.</li>
                    <li><strong>Rushing over expected</strong>: the play-by-play analogue of RYOE (no tracking data), net of down, distance,
                        field position, opponent and the rusher's own line. It describes a season well and projects poorly, so
                        projections do not use it.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Every fitted number is in the <a href={`/pro/file/pro/${PRO_SEASON}/valuation/valuation.pdf`}>valuation methodology PDF</a>.</p>

                <h2 class="fs-4 mt-4">What this doesn't cover</h2>
                <p>Athletic testing, injury history and third-party NIL deals. Dollar values are model estimates, not reported deals.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</GenericPage>
